# Day 03 — Java OOP & Domain Modeling

## Objective

Convert the Campus Lost & Found Portal requirements into Java domain models using Object-Oriented Programming concepts. The domain models represent users, lost reports, found items, claims, handovers, locations, categories, and item statuses that are required for the CampusFound workflow.

---

## Domain Models

### DM-01 — User Model

Create a `User` class to represent Students, Staff members, Security Officers, and Admins.

The model contains:

- User ID
- Name
- Email
- Phone number
- User role

The role is represented using an enum to restrict users to valid system roles.

---

### DM-02 — Lost Report Model

Create a `LostReport` class to represent an item reported as lost.

The model contains:

- Report ID
- Item name
- Category
- Last known location
- Lost date
- Brand
- Color
- Description
- Distinctive markings
- Owner

This model provides the information required by the matching module to identify potential found-item matches.

---

### DM-03 — Found Item Model

Create a `FoundItem` class to represent an item registered as found.

The model contains:

- Item ID
- Item name
- Category
- Found location
- Found date
- Brand
- Color
- Description
- Photo
- Storage locker
- Item status

The item status represents the current stage of the found item lifecycle.

---

### DM-04 — Claim Model

Create a `Claim` class to represent an ownership claim submitted for a found item.

The model contains:

- Claim ID
- Found item
- Claimant
- Verification answers
- Claim status

The claim status can be:

`PENDING` → `APPROVED` / `REJECTED`

This model supports the ownership verification process before an item is released.

---

### DM-05 — Handover Model

Create a `Handover` class to represent the physical release of a verified item.

The model contains:

- Handover ID
- Found item
- Approved claim
- Security officer
- Handover date
- Signature confirmation

The handover model maintains the information required to record the final transfer of custody.

