## OOP Concepts

### Encapsulation

Keep domain-model fields private and provide controlled access through methods.

Example:

`private String itemName;`

`private ItemStatus status;`

This prevents direct modification of important object data.

---

### Abstraction

Expose business operations through meaningful methods instead of directly modifying internal values.

For example:

`approve()`

`reject()`

`recordSignature()`

This keeps the internal implementation of the domain object hidden.

---

### Composition

Represent real-world relationships between CampusFound entities.

Examples:

`LostReport → User`

`LostReport → Location`

`FoundItem → Location`

`Claim → User`

`Claim → FoundItem`

`Handover → Claim`

`Handover → FoundItem`

---

## Item Lifecycle

The domain models support the complete item lifecycle:

`Lost Report`

↓

`Found Item`

↓

`Claim`

↓

`Approved`

↓

`Handover`

↓

`Returned`

For items that remain unclaimed:

`Found Item`

↓

`60 Days`

↓

`Expired`

↓

`Donation / Disposal`

